---
import LegalLayout from "@/layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { LINKS } from "@/consts";
---

<LegalLayout
	title="Code signing policy"
	description="How the Bramble desktop app's Windows installer is built and signed: free code signing provided by SignPath.io, certificate by SignPath Foundation."
	path="/code-signing"
	meta="Effective date: 10 October 2026"
>
	<p class="lead">
		Free code signing provided by <a href="https://signpath.io">SignPath.io</a>, certificate by
		<a href="https://signpath.org">SignPath Foundation</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>What is signed</h2>
	<p>
		The Windows installer for the Bramble desktop app is Authenticode-signed through SignPath. The
		certificate is issued to the SignPath Foundation, so Windows names
		<strong>SignPath Foundation</strong> as the verified publisher when you run the installer. That
		is expected, and it is how you can tell a genuine installer from one that was altered after it
		was signed.
	</p>
	<p>
		Only Bramble's own software is signed this way. Bramble does not use this certificate to sign
		anyone else's programs or libraries.
	</p>

	<h2>How a signed build is made</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>From the public source, on GitHub's machines.</strong> The installer is built by
			GitHub Actions on GitHub-hosted runners, directly from the public
			<a href={LINKS.github}>source repository</a>. Nothing built on a personal computer is ever
			submitted for signing. SignPath verifies where each build came from using information
			supplied by GitHub itself, not by the build.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Every release is approved by hand.</strong> A signing request is only fulfilled
			after an approver (below) reviews and approves it in SignPath.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>The build scripts are source too.</strong> The workflow that builds and signs the
			installer lives in the repository and goes through the same review as the rest of the code.
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Team roles</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Committers and reviewers:</strong>
			<a href="https://github.com/flythenimbus">@flythenimbus</a>. Changes from anyone else arrive
			as pull requests and are reviewed before they are merged.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Approvers:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/flythenimbus">@flythenimbus</a>, who
			approves each signing request.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>Everyone in these roles uses multi-factor authentication for GitHub and for SignPath.</p>

	<h2>Privacy</h2>
	<p>
		Bramble does not transfer any information to other networked systems unless you ask it to, with
		one exception: the desktop app checks for updates when it opens, which you can turn off. Every
		network connection Bramble can make is described in the <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
	</p>

	<h2>Other downloads</h2>
	<p>
		This policy covers the Windows installer, the only download signed through SignPath. Bramble's
		other downloads are signed through their own channels: the macOS app with Bramble's Apple
		Developer ID and notarized by Apple, the browser extensions and the iOS app by their stores, and
		the Android and Linux releases with Bramble's own release keys.
	</p>

	<h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
	<p>
		If you think a signed Bramble installer is not what it should be, or you find a security issue,
		please report it privately through
		<a href={`${LINKS.github}/security/advisories/new`}>GitHub's security advisories</a>.
	</p>
</LegalLayout>
